    "body": "Hi, thanks for releasing the paper and code.\n\nI have a question about the reported speedup numbers, especially the W4A4/W8A8 results in Table 1. The paper reports up to **1.49× speedup** for QVLA under W4A4, while the method assigns different bit-widths `{0, 2, 4, 8, 16}` at a per-output-channel / per-row granularity.\n\nThis is quite surprising from a kernel perspective. In current mixed-precision inference systems, fine-grained mixed precision is usually very hard to make faster than a well-optimized BF16 baseline, and in many cases the extra dequantization, grouping, scattering, or branching overhead makes it slower. Per-channel mixed precision with `{0,2,4,8,16}` seems even harder to accelerate efficiently.\n\nCould you clarify how the speedup in the paper was obtained?\n\nSpecifically:\n\n1. Did you implement a real packed low-bit inference kernel for the mixed `{0,2,4,8,16}` per-channel weight format, or are the weights dequantized back to BF16/FP16 before GEMM?\n\n2. If a real kernel is used, how are channels with different bit-widths executed?\n\n   * Are rows grouped by bit-width and computed with separate GEMMs?\n   * Is dequantization fused into GEMM?\n   * How do you avoid the overhead of per-row metadata, scale/zero-point loading, and output reordering?\n   * How is 2-bit supported?\n\n3. For 0-bit channels, are they structurally pruned so that the GEMM shape is actually reduced, or are they only zeroed out while the original GEMM shape is still computed?\n\n4. Which backend/library was used for the reported speedup?\n\n   * CUTLASS?\n   * TensorRT-LLM?\n   * bitsandbytes?\n   * custom CUDA kernels?\n   * PyTorch fake quantization?\n\n5. The paper says activations are quantized for W4A4/W8A8. Could you point to the activation quantization implementation used in the released code?\n\n6. Could you provide the exact benchmarking script for the BF16 baseline and QVLA, including:\n\n   * warmup / measurement iterations\n   * batch size and sequence length\n   * whether CUDA graph is used\n   * whether `torch.compile`, FlashAttention, or other optimized kernels are enabled\n   * whether both BF16 and QVLA use the same inference stack\n\nThe last point is important because otherwise the reported speedup could simply come from comparing against an under-optimized BF16 baseline. A properly optimized BF16 baseline on RTX 4090 is already quite strong, so achieving 1.49× speedup with such fine-grained mixed precision would be a very interesting kernel result by itself.\n\nRight now, from the released code, it is not obvious where the real low-bit mixed-precision runtime is implemented. If the current code only performs fake quantization or exports dequantized weights, then it would not explain the reported VRAM and speedup numbers in Table 1.\n\nCould you clarify whether the speedup numbers are measured with a real deployed low-bit kernel, or are they estimated / measured under a different implementation that has not been released yet?\n",
